@pqctoday/softhsm-wasm — STATUS NOTICE
======================================

Notice: Educational and demonstration build — not for production use, in any
environment. Not security-audited, not certified, and not intended to protect
real data.

What this package is
--------------------

SoftHSMv3 compiled to WebAssembly, exposing a PKCS#11 v3.2 interface with
ML-KEM, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA. It exists so that people can *learn* and
*experiment* with a post-quantum PKCS#11 token — in a browser or under Node.js
— without provisioning hardware.

What this package is not
------------------------

It is not a hardware security module and it is not a software substitute for
one. In particular:

- It has had no independent security audit and holds no certification. It is
  not FIPS 140-validated, not Common Criteria evaluated, and no result it
  produces is a CAVP/CMVP certificate or an ACVTS verdict.
- Keys it generates live in ordinary process memory (and, in a browser, in
  ordinary browser storage). There is no tamper resistance, no key isolation
  boundary, no secure element, and no guarantee that key material is erased.
- It makes no side-channel claims. Timing, cache and power side channels have
  not been analysed or mitigated, and WebAssembly gives no constant-time
  guarantees.
- Its randomness, key storage, PIN handling, access control and audit
  behaviour are teaching approximations, not a security design.

Therefore: do not use this package, or anything it produces (keys,
certificates, signatures, configurations, exported material), to protect real
data, real users, or real systems. Do not embed it in a product, a service, or
an infrastructure component. Treat every artefact it emits as a teaching
artefact.

This notice is not limited to browser use. The package advertises Node.js
support and can be consumed by a server-side process that never renders any
user interface; the status above applies wherever it runs.

